 ## 2026-11-17
+- **New:** Clips can now be exported in a wide 16:9 YouTube frame (1920x1080) and a square 1:1 frame (1080x1080) as well as the three vertical formats. A vertical clip is fitted whole inside the wider frame -- never stretched or cropped -- with a softly blurred copy of the same picture filling the side or top bands, so burned-in captions stay inside the frame. The wide format follows YouTube's published 1080p upload settings (8 Mbps at 30 fps or below, 12 Mbps above, AAC 384 kbps); square uses the same 1080-class settings. The three existing vertical exports are unchanged.
 - **Fixed:** Renders now use the graphics-card encoder again on newer FFmpeg builds. The startup check handed FFmpeg a filter-graph file option that FFmpeg 8-era builds have removed, so the check failed with "Unrecognized option 'filter_complex_script'" and every render quietly fell back to the processor. The check now passes its graph inline, exactly as production renders already do, and it validates the encoded file before deleting its temporary folder (it previously validated after the folder was gone, which failed on a real FFmpeg even when the encode had worked).
 - **Improved:** Talking videos now get roughly twice the stock B-roll, placed where it helps. Finance, podcast, education, vlog, travel and true-crime clips plan cutaways at 3–4.5 per minute instead of about one per clip, and each cutaway is chosen from what the speaker is actually saying ("interest rates" shows a central bank, not a random building). Words used as names ("Tim Cook", "Steve Jobs") and passing mentions no longer trigger footage. While the speaker says "look at this" the original picture stays on screen, and during a live reaction (laughing, "wow!") the stock shot appears as a framed picture-in-picture so the face stays visible. A stock search that fails because of a rejected key is now recorded as a provider error instead of "nothing found". Set `BROLL_CADENCE=legacy` to keep the previous behaviour.
 - **Improved:** Streamer reaction picking now also reads what the streamer says — exclamations like "no, no, no", "what!" or "bro" in English, Spanish and romanised Hindi — and checks the facecam mouth so in-game character dialogue is less likely to be mistaken for the streamer. Words alone never make a clip: they must line up with a facecam or voice reaction.
